35: \begin{document}
36: \maketitle
37: 
38: \begin{abstract}
39: \noindent
40: This report documents the full development of the \code{compass\_sim} project: a Python simulator for two-dimensional arrays of magnetic compass needles interacting through complete dipolar coupling, with inertial Newtonian dynamics in SI units. The project began as a pedagogical demonstration of the analogy between compass arrays and magnetic materials, including hysteresis and domain formation, and evolved over six development sessions into a research instrument with publication potential. The report records the project decisions, problems encountered and solutions adopted, the code-version history (V20--V56), the research and publication strategy built around a gap identified in the literature, and the results of the pilot campaign on ``damping~$\times$~hysteresis/avalanches''. The pilot already shows the central signal sought: a systematic increase of the avalanche exponent $\tau$ with the quality factor $Q$.
41: \end{abstract}
42: 
43: \tableofcontents
44: \newpage
45: 
46: % ======================================================================
47: \section{Project objective}
48: % ======================================================================
49: 
50: The project now has two layered objectives, reflecting its historical evolution.
51: 
52: \paragraph{Original objective (pedagogical/demonstrative).}
53: To build a visual simulation of an array of compass needles that rotate only under the magnetic field generated by each needle on its neighbors, seeking the collective directional equilibrium. The simulation provides a direct and intuitive mechanical analogy with real magnetic materials, capable of displaying hysteretic behavior and magnetic-domain formation from a top view and exportable as video.
54: 
55: \paragraph{Current objective (scientific).}
56: To use the simulator, which implements real Newtonian \emph{inertial} dynamics, a rare feature in this literature, to address a nonequilibrium statistical-physics question with publication potential:
57: 
58: \begin{quote}\itshape
59: Does the damping coefficient control the universality class of avalanches and the shape of the hysteresis loop in classical dipolar arrays with long-range and anisotropic coupling? Does this response depend on the lattice geometry (square, triangular, honeycomb), that is, on geometric frustration?
60: \end{quote}
61: 
62: The literature has established that underdamped and overdamped systems belong to different universality classes in short-range or mean-field models, such as inertial Kuramoto models, damped Ising models, and sheared amorphous solids~\cite{dahmen2011}. The identified gap is that no one has tested whether this picture survives real dipolar coupling, which is long-ranged ($\sim 1/r^3$) and anisotropic, in two-dimensional arrays with continuous angular dynamics and physical inertia. As far as the literature review reached, the simulator developed here is the only instrument built specifically for this question.
63: 
64: % ======================================================================
65: \section{The physical model}
66: % ======================================================================
67: 
68: Each needle $i$ is represented as a point magnetic dipole of moment $m$ fixed at a lattice position $\vec{r}_i$, free to rotate in the plane around a central pin with no contact friction, but with viscous damping from air. The dynamics is Newton's second law for rotation:
69: \begin{equation}
70: I\,\ddot\theta_i \;=\; \tau_i^{\mathrm{dip}} + \tau_i^{\mathrm{ext}}
71: - b\,\dot\theta_i,
72: \label{eq:eom}
73: \end{equation}
74: where $I$ is the needle's moment of inertia, $b$ is the viscous damping coefficient, and the torques come from $\vec\tau = \vec m \times \vec B$. The dipolar field acting on needle $i$ sums the contributions from \emph{all} other needles, using complete coupling with computational cost $\mathcal{O}(K^2)$ for $K$ needles:
75: \begin{equation}
76: \vec B_j(\vec r_i) = \frac{\mu_0}{4\pi}\,
77: \frac{3(\vec m_j \cdot \hat r)\hat r - \vec m_j}{r^3},
78: \qquad \vec r = \vec r_i - \vec r_j .
79: \end{equation}
80: 
81: The integration uses a Velocity--Verlet scheme with time step $\Delta t$ proportional to the natural period $T_0 = 2\pi/\omega_0$, where
82: \begin{equation}
83: \omega_0 = \sqrt{\frac{m\,B_{\mathrm{ref}}}{I}},
84: \qquad
85: B_{\mathrm{ref}} = \frac{\mu_0}{4\pi}\frac{2m}{r_{nn}^{3}}
86: \end{equation}
87: is the small-oscillation frequency in the reference dipolar field between nearest neighbors, separated by $r_{nn}$. The dynamical regime is characterized by the quality factor
88: \begin{equation}
89: Q = \frac{\omega_0 I}{b},
90: \end{equation}
91: with $Q \gg 1$ underdamped (oscillatory) and $Q \ll 1$ overdamped (monotonic relaxation). This is the central control parameter of the scientific study.
92: 
93: \paragraph{Real physical parameters.}
94: A structuring decision was to use SI units and derive the parameters from the real needle geometry rather than arbitrary constants:
95: \begin{itemize}
96: \item \textbf{Moment of inertia}: computed for a \SI{0.4}{mm}-thick steel sheet shaped as a diamond needle, for example $I \approx \SI{3.8e-11}{kg.m^2}$ and mass $\approx$ \SI{0.017}{g} for a \SI{0.5}{cm} needle;
97: \item \textbf{Magnetic moment}: assuming steel saturated along the long axis, $m = M_s V$, with $M_s = \SI{1.59e6}{A/m}$, corresponding to ordinary carbon steel with $B_{\mathrm{sat}} \approx \SI{2.0}{T}$;
98: \item \textbf{Lattice}: parameterized by the radius $R$ of the circle circumscribing each needle ($r_{nn} = 2R$ in all geometries) and by the fraction \code{needle\_frac} of the diameter occupied by the needle.
99: \end{itemize}
100: Both automatic calculations can be overridden by explicit user parameters.
