179: % ======================================================================
180: \section{Research and publication strategy}
181: \label{sec:research}
182: % ======================================================================
183: 
184: \subsection{The gap in the literature}
185: 
186: \noindent The review covered three fronts. \textbf{(i)}~Classical compass-needle arrays: the founding work (square $22\times22$ lattice, Phys.\ Rev.\ B \textbf{58}, 9238, 1998~\cite{prb1998}) uses equilibrium Monte Carlo with a random field mimicking thermal noise, not Newtonian dynamics. \textbf{(ii)}~Classical dipoles in honeycomb/kagome/triangular lattices (2012--2025): Luttinger--Tisza methods, energy minimization, spin waves, and Monte Carlo with \emph{parallel tempering}, all focused on ground states and thermal \emph{equilibrium} transitions. \textbf{(iii)}~\emph{Artificial spin ice}~\cite{nisoli2013}: real temporal dynamics is studied, but through discrete macrospins (Ising-like) or experimental data (MOKE/MFM), almost never with continuous angular dynamics and explicit mechanical inertia.
187: 
188: The question of how damping controls hysteresis and avalanches is active and publishable in adjacent fields. It is established that underdamped and overdamped regimes form different universality classes in short-range or mean-field systems~\cite{dahmen2011}, but this has not been answered for complete dipolar coupling (long-range, anisotropic) with real spatial geometry. This is the gap occupied by the project.
189: 
190: \subsection{Study design and decision criterion}
191: 
192: The central study sweeps damping over $\sim 2.5$ orders of magnitude in $Q$ (from $Q \approx 15$, underdamped, to $Q \approx 0.05$, overdamped), with multiple initial-noise seeds in the three geometries. It measures hysteresis-loop area, coercive field, remanence, avalanche statistics (power-law exponent $\tau$ in $P(s) \sim s^{-\tau}$, fitted by maximum likelihood using the Clauset--Shalizi--Newman method~\cite{clauset2009}), and domain-size distribution in free relaxation.
193: 
194: The decision criterion was defined \emph{a priori}:
195: \begin{itemize}
196: \item \textbf{Result A (incremental, solid)}: $\tau$ approximately constant with $Q$ (only scales/cutoffs change), consistent with known phenomenology and publishable in the standard target journals;
197: \item \textbf{Result B (surprising)}: $\tau$ varies systematically with $Q$, or there is a qualitative difference between geometries, signaling a breakdown of universality due to anisotropic long-range dipolar coupling. This would justify attempting PRL or a similar venue.
198: \end{itemize}
199: 
200: \subsection{Target journals and APC funding}
201: 
202: \begin{itemize}
203: \item \textbf{CAPES--ACS agreement}: it was confirmed that the CAPES transformative \emph{Read \& Publish} agreement covers \textbf{100\% of the APC in any ACS journal} (CC~BY license) for corresponding authors from consortium institutions, with eligibility checked during submission via ORCID, removing cost as a decision factor;
204: \item \textbf{Primary target}: \emph{ACS Materials Au} (IF~6.5, Q2), the highest-impact fully \emph{open access} ACS option. The paper must be framed as a model for \emph{artificial spin ice} and patterned nanomagnets, which was incorporated into the introduction design;
205: \item \textbf{Backup target}: \emph{ACS Physical Chemistry Au} (IF~4.3, Q2), whose scope explicitly includes condensed matter and is safer in fit;
206: \item \textbf{High-impact alternatives}: \emph{Physical Review Letters} is \emph{outside} the CAPES agreement (APS is not a participating publisher); \emph{Nature Communications}, being \emph{gold OA}, is excluded from \emph{Read \& Publish} agreements (APC $\sim$ USD~6,190 separately). Both are justified only for a type-B result;
207: \item \textbf{Other CAPES publishers} (Springer Nature $\sim$1,750 hybrid titles, Elsevier $\sim$160, Wiley, IEEE, RSP, ACM): a partial review did not find, up to letter~H of the Springer list, a title with better fit than the ACS targets; natural Elsevier candidates (\emph{Physica A}, \emph{JMMM}) await verification in the eligible-title list.
208: \end{itemize}
