106: \section{Development chronology}
107: % ======================================================================
108: 
109: Development took place over six intensive sessions (15--18/06/2026), followed by successive consolidation sessions (04--05/07/2026). This section summarizes the decisions and results from each phase; Table~\ref{tab:versions} consolidates the version record.
110: 
111: \subsection{Phase 1 --- simulation core (15/06)}
112: 
113: The first session established the core: traditional compass needles shaped as diamonds (blue north half, white south half), viewed from above; fully dipolar interaction among all needles; and the search for directional equilibrium. Successive requests added lattice parameters (spacing, $N \times M$ count, square/triangular choice), honeycomb geometry (hexagon vertices), a uniform external field with controllable magnitude and direction, comments documenting all code sections, saving in the current directory, and the video pipeline (PNG frames at regular intervals assembled into MP4 through ffmpeg).
114: 
115: Two important physical decisions were made in this phase: (i) \textbf{SI units} for field and magnetic moment, abandoning arbitrary units; and (ii) \textbf{inertial dynamics}: negligible pin contact friction, but real moment of inertia, so the needle \emph{oscillates} around equilibrium before stabilizing, and this must be visible in the animation. This second decision, originally adopted for visual realism, later became the scientific differential of the project, since inertial dynamics is precisely what is missing in the literature on dipolar arrays.
116: 
117: \subsection{Phase 2 --- control, physical time, and honeycomb geometry (16--17/06)}
118: 
119: This session was dominated by three fronts.
120: 
121: \textbf{(a) Interface and control}: visual indication of external-field direction and intensity in the image; automatic incremental numbering of videos (\code{name0000.mp4}, \code{0001}, \dots); controlled interruption through \mbox{Ctrl+I} or a physical criterion ($S = 1.00$); and explanation/exposure of the damping parameter and the quality factor $Q$.
122: 
123: \textbf{(b) Time semantics}: \code{t\_sim} was redefined to mean the simulated \emph{real physical time}, namely the sum of the integrated $\Delta t$ values, equivalent to what a stopwatch would measure while observing the real system, rather than the wall time of the execution. This distinction led to successive corrections until the behavior became consistent.
124: 
125: \textbf{(c) Honeycomb correction}: the initial implementation produced distorted networks that were not truly hexagonal. After several attempts, the adopted solution was to construct the honeycomb as a triangular lattice with systematic removal of the central site of each hexagon, and to parameterize all geometries by the radius $R$ of the circle circumscribing the needle. This guarantees a uniform $r_{nn} = 2R$ across geometries, which later proved essential for clean scientific comparison among them, with the same nominal $Q$ in all three lattices.
126: 
127: \subsection{Phase 3 --- dynamic-field modes and data export (17/06)}
128: 
129: This phase transformed the demonstrator into a measurement instrument:
130: 
131: \begin{itemize}
132: \item \textbf{Hysteresis mode}: field linearly swept through the full cycle. An important protocol correction was requested and implemented: the correct cycle has \emph{five} segments ($0 \to +B_{\max} \to 0 \to -B_{\max} \to 0 \to +B_{\max}$), not three;
133: \item \textbf{Sinusoidal mode}: $B(t) = B_{\max}\sin(2\pi f t)$ in the chosen direction;
134: \item \textbf{Pulse mode}: field applied until $S > 0.99$ \emph{or} an $S$ plateau is reached (variation $<5\%$ over 10 iterations), then set to zero to observe free relaxation. The plateau criterion was added after the saturation-only criterion proved insufficient;
135: \item \textbf{CSV export}: $(t, B, M, S)$ series with magnetization projected along the field direction, using the same numbering scheme as the videos. This became the backbone of the subsequent scientific analysis;
136: \item Robustness fixes: degenerate case $N = M = 1$, terminal output with corrupted line feeds, and image resolution for large arrays ($\geq 20 \times 20$).
137: \end{itemize}
138: 
139: Periodic boundary conditions (PBC) were introduced at the end of this phase as an on/off parameter, then completed in the next phase with image replicas in the dipolar-field calculation (default: one copy in each direction).
